# Game manuals, field drawings and CAD downloaded from FIRST.
#
# Excluded for two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient. They are not ours to
# redistribute: they are FIRST's copyrighted material, and a public mirror of them is a
# redistribution however well intentioned. And they are 245 MB of this folder's 270 MB, which
# makes every clone of this repository pay for files anybody can download from FIRST directly.
#
# Everything that reads them still works on a machine that has them. Nothing in this repository
# depends on them being version controlled.
manuals/

# Manual text, rule bodies, tables and page renders extracted from FIRST's documents.
#
# Excluded for the first reason above: an extract is still FIRST's copyrighted material, whether
# it is a manual's flat text, its rule bodies or a render of one of its pages. None of it needs
# to be version controlled. tools/RUN-KICKOFF.sh regenerates the bundles from a manual you
# download from FIRST.
#
# Each analysis/<label>/bundle/ holds one competition manual's full flat text, its rule bodies
# and orange boxes, its tables, the grep lines in TRIPWIRES.txt and renders of its pages, plus
# small derived lists that mean nothing without the rest. The script deletes and rebuilds the
# directory on every run, so a file left tracked inside it would show as deleted or modified
# after the next one.
#
# The second line repeats the exclusion for the page renders in figures/. A render is a whole
# manual page, logos included (FIRST's, the game's and the sponsors'), so it is a trademark
# question as well as a copyright one, and it stays out even if the first line is narrowed.
analysis/*/bundle/
analysis/*/bundle/figures/

# The same extracts written outside a bundle. Without --out, extract-tables.py, rule-bodies.py
# and render-pages.py write tables_*, rulebodies_* and figures_* next to the input PDF. A manual
# dropped in this folder for kickoff day (README.md section 4) stays here, because
# tools/ingest-manual.sh copies it into manuals/ rather than moving it, and those tools pointed
# at it write their output here too. render-pages.py names every page render
# pNNN_<section>.png; the last line catches a render copied anywhere else.
/*.pdf
/tables_*/
/rulebodies_*/
/figures_*/
p[0-9][0-9][0-9]_*.png

# Run leftovers tools/RUN-KICKOFF.sh writes to analysis/<label>/ beside the bundle.
#
# _guard.tsv and _guard.txt are the parser's rule list and log for the check that tells a
# kickoff manual from the pre-season V0. The script deletes them at the end of a run that passes
# that check, so a refused manual (exit 3 or 4) or a run stopped partway leaves them behind,
# holding the ID, page, class and headline of every rule in the refused PDF. fetch.log is the
# output of tools/kickoff-fetch.sh, written whenever the script finds no manual locally and goes
# to get one, and nothing deletes it. None of it is analysis; it records one machine's attempt.
analysis/*/_guard.*
analysis/*/fetch.log

# Raw inputs in _workfiles/: plain text of FIRST's Team Updates and official Q&A archive.
#
# These are FIRST's copyrighted text as well, and nothing in this repository reads them. The
# folder is excluded by default so a future extract stays unpublished whatever its name;
# _workfiles/README.txt gives the pdftotext command that regenerates each one. The Chief Delphi
# category dump stays out too: it carries forum members' post excerpts and usernames, and
# nothing reads it either. The negation that keeps README.txt must stay below the line it
# overrides. That line has to remain _workfiles/* rather than _workfiles/, because git cannot
# re-include a file inside an excluded directory. The first two lines name today's extracts and
# the dump; they are redundant while the folder line stands and keep those files out if it is
# ever narrowed.
_workfiles/*_text.txt
_workfiles/chiefdelphi_category_list.json
_workfiles/*
!_workfiles/README.txt

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~

# Python bytecode and editor backups.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.bak
*.bak[0-9]
